==========================================================================================================================
PART 0 -- the disc geometry factor, computed exactly (bug patterns 1 and 2 closed before any data)
==========================================================================================================================
       R/R_d   eps = g_bar R^2/(G M_d)   what a point mass (eps=1) would get wrong
       1.000                    0.2780   -0.556 dex
       2.000                    0.7715   -0.113 dex
       3.356                    1.1344   +0.055 dex
       4.000                    1.1890   +0.075 dex
       6.000                    1.1667   +0.067 dex
      10.000                    1.0584   +0.025 dex
  [PASS] k03-0 the enclosed-mass geometry is the exact exponential disc, not a total mass over a radius squared (hunt bug pattern 1) and not a sphere (pattern 2); at R = 2 R_e = 3.356 R_d the disc gives eps = 1.1344, so treating it as a point mass would misplace g_bar by +0.055 dex and, at LAMBDA ~ -1.2, a_0 by the same again   (eps(2 R_e) = 1.1344 = +0.0548 dex -- a flattened disc pulls HARDER than a point mass of the same total at this radius, and the correction is load-bearing at the 0.05 dex level)

==========================================================================================================================
PART 1 -- MUSE-DARK II (Jeanneau+2026), the only high-z sample observed in the deep-MOND regime
==========================================================================================================================
  N = 95 at z = 0.50 - 1.45 (median 1.03); lensing magnification median 2.3
  median log M* = 9.12, log M_bar = 9.92, gas fraction = 0.70
  median y = g_bar/a_0 = 0.330 (canonical) / 0.273 (alt);  median LAMBDA = -1.70
  71 of 95 have g_obs > g_bar and therefore invert; 24 do not (they sit BELOW the framework's own floor and are reported, not dropped silently)

    AGAINST MY OWN FIRST NUMBER: the repository's frozen cut quotes a median g_bar/a_0 of 0.16 for its
    deep-61 subsample, but that g_bar is obtained by INVERTING g_obs through the kernel.  Built
    INDEPENDENTLY from the catalogue's own M_bar and the exact disc geometry, the median is higher.
  [PASS] k03-1 THE FINDING THAT REOPENS THE REDSHIFT AXIS, and it corrects k02's own Part 4: a high-redshift sample observed near the deep-MOND regime DOES exist.  Gravitational lensing of low-mass star-forming galaxies puts MUSE-DARK II an order of magnitude lower in y than RC100 or MSA-3D, halving the baryonic-mass amplification   (median y = 0.330 (canonical), LAMBDA = -1.70, against RC100's y = 1.96, LAMBDA = -3.36; 29/95 pass the strict |LAMBDA| <= 1.5 gate that admitted 0/100 of RC100 and 0/23 of MSA-3D)

    THE CENSORING LIABILITY, stated before any a_0 is quoted.  A galaxy inverts only if g_obs > g_bar,
    i.e. only if it sits ABOVE the framework's own baryons-only floor.  Those that do not are a
    one-sided censoring at the BOTTOM of the a_0 distribution, so the median of the inverting subset is
    an OVER-estimate of the sample's a_0.
       censored fraction 0.253 (24 of 95);  <z> censored 1.14 vs inverting 0.92;  <log M_bar> censored 10.00 vs 9.84
  [PASS] k03-1b THE LIABILITY: a quarter of MUSE-DARK II sits BELOW the framework's own baryons-only floor at 2 R_e once its catalogued baryonic mass and the exact disc geometry are used -- g_bar exceeds g_obs and no a_0 exists.  Either those scaling-relation gas masses are too large or those velocities are too small; either way the level quoted below is censored and upper-leaning   (24/95 = 25.3% have g_obs < g_bar; the censoring is one-sided and is carried into Part 2 as a censored median)

