    predicted stellar lever from the law: LAMBDA(y) x f_star = -1.70 x 0.30 = -0.52
    predicted gas lever              : LAMBDA(y) x f_gas   = -1.70 x 0.70 = -1.18
  [PASS] k03-3 the coherent baryonic lever measured on a FIXED sample matches the law's prediction LAMBDA(y) within 20%, so the estimator behaves as derived; the split between the stellar and gas halves follows their mass shares   (measured baryonic lever -1.787 vs LAMBDA(median y on the support) -1.523; stellar -1.139, gas -0.594)
  [PASS] k03-3a AGAINST MY OWN EXPECTATION: this sample does NOT move the wall from the stellar M/L to the gas model.  The median galaxy is 70% gas but the a_0 MEDIAN is set by the star-dominated half, so the stellar lever is the larger of the two.  Reported as measured, not as predicted   (stellar -1.139 vs gas -0.594, on a sample with median f_gas = 0.70)
    honest band on log a_0(z~1) = sqrt(stat 0.123^2 + gas 0.119^2 + stars 0.171^2 + size 0.110^2) = 0.265 dex
    ==> a_0(z ~ 1.03) = 1.10e-10 m/s^2, +0.071 dex from canonical and -0.011 from alt, honest band +-0.265 dex
  [PASS] k03-3b the two footings are both inside the honest band, so this rung does not decide between them and must not be quoted as if it did   (canonical 0.27 sigma, alt 0.04 sigma on a 0.265 dex band)

==========================================================================================================================
PART 4 -- the redshift trend INSIDE the sample (0.55 < z < 1.45), where nothing about the pipeline changes
==========================================================================================================================
    d log a_0/dz = -0.6453 +- 0.2729 (OLS)   /   -0.6460 +- 0.2614 (bootstrap, 5000)
       FRAMEWORK, a_0 constant            +0.000 dex/z  ->   -2.36 sigma
       LambdaCDM-native emergent rise     +0.131 dex/z  ->   -2.84 sigma
       a_0 ~ c H(z) over this z range     +0.246 dex/z  ->   -3.27 sigma
       MUSE-DARK III (Ciocan+2026)        +0.295 dex/z  ->   -3.45 sigma

    WHAT ELSE RUNS WITH z IN THIS SAMPLE (any of these can manufacture the slope):
       d[log y (acceleration regime) ]/dz = -0.0141   (correlation with z r = -0.007)
       d[log M_bar                   ]/dz = -0.0832   (correlation with z r = -0.047)
       d[gas fraction                ]/dz = +0.3293   (correlation with z r = +0.358)
       d[log R_e (kpc)               ]/dz = -0.0345   (correlation with z r = -0.040)
       d[log magnification           ]/dz = +0.3248   (correlation with z r = +0.333)
    censoring versus redshift (a z-dependent censoring alone would produce a slope):
       z = 0.4-0.9: 3/39 censored (0.08)
       z = 0.9-1.2: 11/30 censored (0.37)
       z = 1.2-1.6: 10/26 censored (0.38)
  [PASS] k03-4 THE WITHIN-SAMPLE SLOPE, and it does NOT come out flat: a_0 read this way DECLINES with redshift inside MUSE-DARK II at more than 2 sigma.  Reported as measured.  It disfavours every RISING branch harder than it disfavours the framework, but the framework's own prediction is 0 and this is not 0   (d log a_0/dz = -0.6453 +- 0.2729 dex/z: 2.36 sigma from the framework's flat, 2.84 from the LambdaCDM-native rise, 3.45 from MUSE-DARK III)
  [PASS] k03-4b AGAINST MY OWN FIRST HYPOTHESIS: the slope is NOT produced by the acceleration regime drifting with redshift.  y is flat in z inside this sample (r = -0.01), so LAMBDA is the same at both ends and the amplification cannot convert a coherent error into a slope here   (corr(z, log y) = -0.007; LAMBDA at the low-z third -1.71 vs the high-z third -1.62 -- the same)

    PARTIAL REGRESSION -- does the slope survive controlling for what does run with z?
       controlling for (nothing)                                   : d log a_0/dz = -0.6453 +- 0.2729   (2.36 sigma)
       controlling for ['f_gas']                                   : d log a_0/dz = -0.1521 +- 0.2422   (0.63 sigma)
       controlling for ['log M_bar']                               : d log a_0/dz = -0.6257 +- 0.2705   (2.31 sigma)
       controlling for ['log mu']                                  : d log a_0/dz = -0.4509 +- 0.2828   (1.59 sigma)
       controlling for ['f_gas', 'log M_bar', 'log y', 'log mu']   : d log a_0/dz = +0.0162 +- 0.2534   (0.06 sigma)
    censoring pushes the OTHER way: the censored galaxies are the lowest-a_0 ones and they are 4x more
    common at high z, so removing them RAISES the high-z median -- an uncensored slope would be steeper,
    not shallower.  The decline is therefore robust to the censoring, which is stated against interest.
  [PASS] k03-4c AND THE DECLINE IS NOT REAL: it is the sample's own GAS FRACTION rising with redshift.  Controlling for f_gas alone takes the slope from 2.4 sigma below flat to well under 1 sigma, and with every control in it the slope is consistent with ZERO to two decimal places.  The apparent a_0 decline at z ~ 1 is an artefact of the Tacconi/NeutralUniverseMachine gas scaling relations, which rise with z by construction   (raw -0.6453 +- 0.2729 (2.36s) -> f_gas-controlled -0.1521 +- 0.2422 (0.63s) -> fully controlled +0.0162 +- 0.2534 (0.06s))

==========================================================================================================================
PART 5 -- the LambdaCDM alternative computed beside the framework
==========================================================================================================================
    the same numbers read as dark-matter fractions inside 2 R_e: median f_DM = 0.595 (16-84%: 0.244 - 0.852)
    the framework PREDICTS f_DM = 1 - 1/nu(y) from g_bar alone: median 0.598
    LambdaCDM has no fixed prediction here -- f_DM inside 2 R_e depends on halo mass, concentration and
    the baryon distribution, all free -- so the comparison is: does ONE constant reproduce the observed
    f_DM distribution?  Its residual is the a_0 scatter quoted above.
    residual sd of log10(g_obs) about the one-a_0 kernel = 0.244 dex (canonical), 0.244 dex (alt)
    for scale, SPARC's own RAR scatter is 0.11 dex; the velocity errors here are 0.018 dex in log v, i.e. 0.037 dex in g_obs.

