  [PASS] M1 z-shuffle: permuting the redshifts must destroy the slope, and it does -- so the slope in Part 4 is a redshift trend and not an artefact of the sample's own spread   (real -0.6453, shuffled -0.0094 +- 0.2797)
  [PASS] M2 mutation: nu = 1 (baryons only, no boost) must be worse than the kernel, and it is -- but AGAINST INTEREST the margin is a factor of six in chi2, not orders of magnitude, because a sample at y ~ 0.3 is only boosted by a factor ~2.5 and its velocity errors are small compared with its intrinsic scatter   (chi2(nu=1) = 2.31e+05 vs chi2(kernel at the fitted a_0) = 3.76e+04 on 71 points (ratio 6.1); residual rms 0.322 dex vs 0.244 dex)
  [PASS] M3 injection closure at log a_0 = canonical +0.00 dex: the estimator returns it exactly   (injected -10.0287, recovered -10.0287)
  [PASS] M3 injection closure at log a_0 = canonical +0.20 dex: the estimator returns it exactly   (injected -9.8287, recovered -9.8287)
  [PASS] M4 the bug-pattern control, quantified: using the TOTAL baryonic mass as a point mass at 2 R_e (hunt bug patterns 1 and 2 together) shifts the measured a_0 by a definite amount, so the disc geometry is load-bearing and is not a detail   (point-mass a_0 = 1.235e-10 vs disc a_0 = 1.102e-10 (+0.050 dex))
    footing canonical : median y = 0.330, median LAMBDA = -1.70, gate fraction 0.31
    footing alt       : median y = 0.273, median LAMBDA = -1.63, gate fraction 0.37
  [PASS] M5 both footings: the sample sits in the same regime on either footing, so the regime claim -- the one thing this rung rests on -- is footing-independent   (median y 0.330 (canonical) vs 0.273 (alt), gate fraction 0.31 / 0.37)
    gas stress HI x0.5: a_0 = 1.283e-10 (+0.066 dex)
    gas stress HI x2.0: a_0 = 1.261e-10 (+0.058 dex)

==========================================================================================================================
VERDICT (k03)
==========================================================================================================================
  MUSE-DARK II is the only high-redshift sample in this repository's archive observed where a_0 is
  measurable with a lever near -1: median
  y = 0.33, LAMBDA = -1.70, against RC100's -3.4.  Gravitational lensing of low-mass
  star-forming galaxies is therefore the design for the decisive z ~ 2.5 test, not massive discs.
  a_0(z ~ 1.03) = 1.10e-10 m/s^2, +0.07 dex from canonical / -0.01 from alt, honest band +-0.27 dex.
  Against the SAME estimator at z ~ 0 (9.63e-11): +0.058 +- 0.125 dex (stat), 0.5 sigma -- one a_0 across 8 Gyr.
  AGAINST INTEREST, three ways: (i) a quarter of the sample sits BELOW the framework's own baryons-only
  floor, so the level is censored and upper-leaning (censored median 5.12e-11, -0.26 dex from canonical);
  (ii) the RAW within-sample slope is -0.65 +- 0.27 dex/z, 2.4 sigma BELOW flat -- but it does NOT
  survive: controlling for the sample's own gas fraction, which rises with z by construction in the
  Tacconi/NUM scaling relations, leaves +0.016 +- 0.253 dex/z, i.e. FLAT;
  (iii) the honest band on the level is +-0.27 dex, so no footing is decided.
  What the raw slope DOES do is sit far from every RISING branch on the same data that produced the
  rising claim: MUSE-DARK III's +0.295 dex/z is 3.4 sigma away and a_0 ~ cH(z) is 3.3 sigma away.
  NOT Kepler-grade at this precision -- but this is the sample class the decisive z ~ 2.5 test should use.
==========================================================================================================================

